59	\begin{abstract}
60	The coherent manner in which Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) forms an image unavoidably imprints speckle onto it, and this noise sharply lowers image quality. Current global-modeling denoisers, however, over-smooth isolated strong scatterers and fail to cleanly separate speckle from high-frequency texture in the spatial domain. To address these problems, we introduce the Receptance Weighted Key Value (RWKV) mechanism into the field of SAR image denoising and propose a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a sparse-attention-enhanced RWKV network (SAR-RWKV). First, to solve the blooming of high-brightness targets caused by global information aggregation, SAR-RWKV constructs a sparse-gated spatial-mixing RWKV module that dynamically locks onto and isolates important high-coherence point targets through a Top-K mechanism, achieving long-range background denoising while better preserving local strong-scattering features. Second, to address the severe aliasing between speckle and real terrain details in the spatial domain, a Channel Frequency Mixing (CFM) module is designed for SAR-RWKV, which maps features into the frequency domain through the discrete cosine transform. The sparsity of the frequency-domain representation is exploited to decouple speckle noise from texture, and the frequency components are adaptively weighted according to the content, significantly enhancing the restoration of image texture details. In addition, a Multi-Scale Total Variation (MSTV) loss is incorporated into SAR-RWKV to overcome the over-smoothing caused by the traditional total variation loss. Experiments on multiple SAR image datasets show that SAR-RWKV outperforms a variety of state-of-the-art methods in both quantitative and qualitative evaluations.
61	\end{abstract}
62	
