64	SAR image; Speckle reduction; Receptance Weighted Key Value; Multiscale total variation.
65	\end{IEEEkeywords}
66	\section{Introduction}
67	\IEEEPARstart{A}{s} an active microwave remote sensing imaging technology, Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) has been widely used in environmental monitoring, disaster assessment, military reconnaissance, resource exploration, and other fields, owing to its all-day, all-weather imaging capability and its ability to penetrate clouds and fog \cite{ref1,ref2}. Compared with optical remote sensing, SAR can obtain high-quality surface information under severe weather conditions, providing key data support for time-sensitive emergency response \cite{ref3}. However, the coherent imaging mechanism of SAR systems introduces speckle noise, which seriously hinders the interpretation of ground information. Speckle is an inherent physical phenomenon arising from the coherent superposition of randomly distributed scatterers within a resolution cell \cite{ref4}. It appears as a spatially correlated grainy random pattern with multiplicative and non-stationary statistical properties, which severely degrades the radiometric resolution and visual interpretability of images \cite{ref5,ref6}. Speckle noise not only blurs object edges and geometric contours but also destroys texture and structural information, significantly affecting the performance of downstream tasks such as object detection \cite{ref7,ref8}, terrain classification \cite{ref9}, and change detection \cite{ref10}. Therefore, how to effectively suppress speckle noise while recovering more image details has long been a core challenge in the field of SAR image processing.
68	
69	Traditional SAR image denoising methods have evolved from spatial-domain filtering to transform-domain filtering. Early spatial-domain methods are mainly based on local statistical models and suppress noise by adaptive weighted averaging; representative examples include the Lee, Frost, Kuan, enhanced Lee, and Gamma-MAP filters \cite{ref11,ref12}. The Lee filter performs adaptive filtering using local mean and variance estimates, but it is prone to over-smoothing at image edges and in textured regions. The Frost filter averages spatial pixels with an exponentially decaying weighting function; it performs well in homogeneous regions but has difficulty preserving complex image structures. The Kuan filter improves the weight computation of the Lee filter by introducing the coefficient of variation, but it remains subject to the assumption of local stationarity. The enhanced Lee and Gamma-MAP filters further account for the Gamma distribution of speckle and improve edge preservation, but they are sensitive to window size and computationally inefficient. The common limitation of these methods is their reliance on hand-crafted local statistical models, which cannot adapt to the diversity of complex scenes. Spatial-domain denoising methods based on non-local self-similarity significantly improve denoising performance by mining the globally redundant information of the image. Non-local means filtering \cite{ref13} breaks through the limitation of local filtering by performing weighted averaging based on the similarity between image patches. Deledalle et al. \cite{ref14} proposed a patch-matching algorithm based on a probabilistic similarity measure, which extends the similarity measure to the multiplicative noise model of SAR images and achieves good denoising results. Sun et al. \cite{ref15} proposed a region-guided non-local means SAR image denoising algorithm; by partitioning the image into different regions and computing the weights of non-local similar patches in region-specific ways according to the local statistical characteristics of the SAR image, the algorithm effectively suppresses noise while better protecting image edges. However, because non-local means (NLM) based methods must match similar patches in a high-dimensional search space, their computational complexity is high, and strong scattering points tend to spread in heavily speckled areas. Transform-domain methods map the noisy image into a transform domain and shrink its coefficients to achieve denoising. Among traditional methods, the wavelet transform \cite{ref16} can separate noise through multi-resolution analysis, but its isotropic basis functions can hardly characterize the complex directional features of SAR images. Although the Shearlet \cite{ref17} and Curvelet \cite{ref18} transforms enhance edge characterization by introducing direction-sensitive bases, they introduce artificial textures during SAR image denoising.
70	
71	The rise of deep learning provides a new paradigm for SAR image denoising. Through hierarchical feature learning and end-to-end training, Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) can automatically learn complex noise-signal mappings from data and are therefore widely used in SAR image denoising. For example, SAR-CNN, proposed by Chierchia et al. \cite{ref19}, directly applies an optical image denoising network to SAR images. Although it achieves some success, its generalization is limited because it simulates speckle with additive white Gaussian noise, neglecting the multiplicative nature and spatial correlation of speckle. To address this issue, Wang et al. \cite{ref20} proposed ID-CNN, which explicitly models log-domain additive noise by transforming the image into log-space and estimates the speckle by combining convolution, batch normalization, the Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) activation function, and residual layers, achieving a better denoising effect. SAR-DRN, proposed by Zhang et al. \cite{ref21}, introduces dilated convolution, which achieves a better denoising effect with a lightweight structure while keeping the filter size and layer depth unchanged; in addition, to preserve image details and mitigate the vanishing-gradient problem, the method incorporates skip connections and a residual learning strategy into the denoising model. Liu et al. \cite{ref22} proposed a SAR image denoising algorithm based on CNN and guided filtering, which achieves blind denoising by training at different noise levels and combining a two-stage image fusion based on guided filtering, obtaining good generalization performance; however, it requires training separate models for different noise levels, which slows training. Vitale et al. \cite{ref23} designed a noise suppression model that combines a multi-objective loss function with a CNN, effectively balancing texture preservation and edge sharpening. Although these methods have made significant progress, the local receptive field of CNNs limits their ability to model the long-range spatial dependencies of SAR images. Even if the receptive field is expanded by deepening the network or using dilated convolution, the effective receptive field still decays exponentially and performs poorly on large-scale textures and complex scenes \cite{ref24}.
72	
73	To overcome the limitations of CNNs, the Transformer architecture based on the self-attention mechanism has been introduced into the field of SAR image denoising. For example, Liu et al. \cite{ref25} proposed a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a multi-scale residual dense attention network, which effectively balances denoising and texture preservation. Shen et al. \cite{ref26} proposed a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a dynamic adaptive Transformer, which uses a dynamic gated attention module to characterize the spatial heterogeneity of noise and optimizes the high- and low-frequency components through a frequency-domain multi-expert enhancement module, achieving adaptive suppression of multi-source mixed noise. However, since the computational complexity and memory consumption of the standard self-attention mechanism scale quadratically with the sequence length, such models face a severe efficiency challenge when processing high-resolution SAR images \cite{ref27}. To solve this problem, Kundu et al. \cite{ref28} proposed a linear-angular attention Transformer, which achieves global context capture with linear time complexity in a hybrid framework by improving the way attention is computed. Model complexity can also be reduced by window partitioning, sparse attention, and other techniques, but this often sacrifices the global modeling ability of the model. How to achieve truly global modeling while maintaining linear computational complexity, and how to denoise SAR images in accordance with the particular characteristics of SAR speckle noise, remain key open issues. The Receptance Weighted Key Value (RWKV) architecture provides a new way to solve these problems \cite{ref29}. RWKV ingeniously reformulates the attention mechanism into a recursive form, enabling global sequence modeling with linear complexity through temporal and channel mixing mechanisms. Specifically, RWKV leverages learnable position encoding and recursive state updates so that the representation of each position can aggregate all historical information while keeping computation and memory growth linear. RWKV has gradually been applied to various image processing tasks. For example, Yang et al. \cite{ref30} proposed Restore-RWKV, an image restoration method that introduces the RWKV mechanism into image processing by using spatial mixing to emulate temporal mixing, achieving superior performance in medical image denoising.
74	
75	Based on the above analysis, we introduce the RWKV mechanism, combined with frequency-domain analysis, into SAR image denoising and propose SAR-RWKV, a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a sparse-attention-enhanced RWKV network, to achieve efficient, high-quality SAR image denoising. Specifically, to break the bottleneck whereby global modeling causes blooming of isolated bright targets, we abandon the conventional fully connected spatial mixing and construct a sparse-gated spatial-mixing RWKV module, which makes the network focus on extracting salient local features while maintaining fine-grained details, realizing the complementary advantages of global information and sparse features. 
76	
77	(1) A global modeling network architecture with linear complexity: we introduce the RWKV mechanism into the SAR image denoising task for the first time and construct a spatial-mixing RWKV architecture based on sparse attention. During spatial mixing, a Top-K sparse filtering strategy is applied to the key component to strengthen the model's response to critical sparse regions and improve the effectiveness of global modeling. At the physical level of SAR imagery, the significance of this architecture is that it can adaptively separate the redundant information of the homogeneous background from the structural priors of high-frequency regions, thus effectively preventing over-smoothing of strong-scattering-point features while suppressing multiplicative speckle globally.
78	
79	(2) The Channel Frequency Mixing (CFM) module: In improving RWKV to address the severe aliasing between speckle and real texture in the spatial domain, we construct a CFM module through a Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT)-based frequency-domain enhancement module that weights different frequency components in a content-adaptive manner, making full use of the sparsity of the frequency-domain representation to enhance the model's ability to recover texture details.
80	
81	(3) The Multi-Scale Total Variation (MSTV) loss: we design a spatially adaptive weight-generation mechanism that combines local entropy estimation with gradient-magnitude information, so that homogeneous regions receive stronger regularization to fully suppress speckle while textured regions retain more detail. A parallel multi-scale convolution structure is also employed to effectively model the local statistical characteristics of features at different scales, thereby overcoming the over-smoothing caused by the traditional total variation loss and achieving a balance between noise suppression and detail preservation.
82	
83	Extensive experiments on simulated SAR datasets and real SAR images show that SAR-RWKV significantly outperforms both traditional methods and the latest deep learning methods across quantitative metrics and visual quality, validating its effectiveness and superiority. The rest of this article is organized as follows: Section 2 briefly reviews related work. Section 3 describes the network architecture and key module designs in detail. Section 4 presents the experimental setup, datasets, and evaluation metrics, reports the experimental results, and provides an in-depth analysis. Section 5 concludes the paper.
84	
85	\section{Related Works}
86	Owing to their simple noise assumptions and local convolution operations, CNN-based methods have difficulty fully describing the complex non-stationary speckle characteristics and long-range spatial dependencies in SAR images. To overcome these limitations, SAR image denoising algorithms based on hybrid convolution-attention frameworks have been widely adopted. For example, Yin et al. \cite{ref31} proposed a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a dual-branch residual sparse network, which builds a series-parallel structure, fuses residual sparse blocks with attention-guidance modules, and jointly models the structural and textural information of images at different scales, significantly improving denoising performance. Liu et al. \cite{ref32} proposed a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a local-global dual-branch network: the local branch extracts fine-grained features through a convolutional hybrid attention module, while the global branch uses the self-attention mechanism to model long-range dependencies, and the complementary fusion of the two branches enhances feature representation. Liu et al. \cite{ref33} also designed a denoising model based on a feature-refinement attention network, comprising a three-stage framework of a feature-refinement network, an attention encoder-decoder, and global residual learning, which progressively optimizes the denoising result through multi-level processing. To further improve local texture preservation and edge protection, Perera et al. \cite{ref34} constructed a speckle suppression network based on a Transformer encoder, which establishes associations between different image regions through the self-attention mechanism and improves the consistency of noise suppression. From another perspective, Fang et al. \cite{ref35} introduced contrastive learning into the hybrid denoising framework, enhancing the network's feature discrimination by distinguishing positive and negative sample pairs and achieving better denoising performance without large amounts of paired data. Mao et al. \cite{ref36} proposed a supervised collaborative denoising method that combines alternating optimization with an attention mechanism: it progressively optimizes the denoised image quality using an alternating conjugate gradient method and introduces a dynamic edge-guided attention module that steers network decisions through adaptive weights, improving feature learning and image-detail extraction.
87	
88	Although the above methods have achieved some success in their respective scenarios, limitations remain. While denoising methods based on hybrid convolution-attention frameworks possess global modeling capability, they face an efficiency bottleneck when processing high-resolution SAR images, because the computational complexity of attention—especially self-attention in the Transformer architecture—grows quadratically with image size. In recent years, the RWKV architecture has attracted increasing attention as a new sequence modeling method. By introducing time-decay and state-update mechanisms, RWKV reduces the computational complexity of self-attention from quadratic to linear while retaining the ability to model long-range dependencies. This gives it a clear efficiency advantage on high-resolution images; accordingly, we combine the RWKV model with frequency-domain analysis to construct a SAR image denoising model based on sparse RWKV.
89	
90	In addition, the speckle noise of SAR images exhibits different characteristics in the spatial and frequency domains, yet most existing methods operate in a single domain, making it difficult to fully exploit multi-domain information for noise suppression. With the rise of deep learning, researchers have gradually combined transform-domain techniques with deep learning to denoise SAR images. For example, Baraha et al. \cite{ref37} proposed a speckle suppression model based on improved anisotropic diffusion and the wavelet transform, which combines the discrete wavelet transform, dynamic weighted adaptive thresholding, weighted least squares, and guided filtering for noise suppression, showing stronger robustness in preserving edge and structural information. Liu et al. \cite{ref38} proposed a mixed-phase speckle suppression algorithm that combines Shearlet-based sparse representation with a CNN; it effectively suppresses the generation of artificial textures but is computationally expensive. Saleem et al. \cite{ref39} proposed a noise suppression algorithm combining a CNN with a log-domain frequency-aware loss, which significantly improves the model's ability to suppress multiplicative noise. Meanwhile, Ni et al. \cite{ref40} proposed a denoising architecture based on phase-amplitude cross-domain collaboration to meet the needs of target perception in complex scenes; by explicitly decoupling the phase and amplitude components of complex signals and performing heterogeneous interactive reconstruction in the transform domain, it significantly enhances noise suppression and feature extraction for small targets against non-stationary backgrounds. Inspired by these algorithms, we construct a CFM module, which enhances the model's ability to restore image texture details through its AFM module.
91	
92	Finally, how to design a loss function that not only exploits the powerful representation capability of deep networks but also effectively integrates the physical priors and local statistical properties of SAR remains an open question. Previous deep learning methods mainly use the L1 or L2 loss to directly measure the pixel-level difference between the predicted image and the Ground Truth (GT). However, speckle noise has multiplicative characteristics, a Gamma distribution, and non-stationary local statistics, which are difficult to constrain with a simple L1 or L2 loss. To better preserve image structure and texture, researchers have introduced various prior constraints, among which the total variation loss based on partial differential equations is widely used in traditional denoising methods \cite{ref41}. Based on the piecewise-smooth assumption of natural images, the total variation loss achieves noise suppression and edge preservation by minimizing the L1 norm of the image gradient, and some works combine it with deep learning as an auxiliary constraint to guide network training. However, the standard total variation loss imposes a uniform smoothness constraint over the whole image without considering the differing statistical characteristics of different regions; the constraint may be insufficient in homogeneous regions or too strong in textured regions, and it easily produces staircase artifacts—all of which is inconsistent with the non-stationarity and local statistical differences of SAR images. For SAR image denoising, designing a loss function that accounts for the Gamma distribution, spatial correlation, and non-stationarity of speckle is therefore an important research direction. An ideal loss function should impose stronger smoothness constraints in homogeneous (low local variance) regions to adequately suppress speckle, preserve details in texture and edge (high local variance or gradient) regions to avoid over-smoothing, and accommodate the different constraint requirements of multi-scale structures. Accordingly, we introduce the MSTV loss to overcome the over-smoothing caused by the traditional total variation loss and to achieve an optimal balance between noise suppression and detail preservation.
93	\vspace{-2pt}
94	\section{Methodology}
95	\subsection{Overall Framework}
96	SAR-RWKV is mainly composed of a convolution operation, a Patch Embedding (PE) layer, an RWKV Mixture (RWKVM) module, a residual connection and Patch Unembedding (PUNE) layer, and Fig. 1 depicts its overall architecture. A single convolutional layer first lifts the grayscale input into a high-dimensional feature space; the PE layer then serializes the two-dimensional features and sends them to the RWKVM module for deep feature modeling, that is:
97	\begin{equation}
98		F _ {0} = P E \left({Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left(I _ {{input}}\right)\right)\tag{1}
99	\end{equation}
100	\begin{equation}
101	F _ {i} = R W K V M _ {i} \left(F _ {i - 1}\right) + F _ {i - 1}, \quad i = 1, 2, 3, 4\tag{2}
102	\end{equation}
103	where $I_{input} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 64 \times 64}$ is the input image, $Conv_{3 \times 3}(\cdot)$ denotes the convolution operation, $PE(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the PE layer, and $F_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{C \times H \times W}$ is the shallow feature. $C$  is the embedding dimension. $F_i$ denotes the feature output by the $i$-th RWKVM module, and  $RWKVM_i(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the RWKVM module. Subsequently, SAR-RWKV then restores the features to the original scale through the PUNE layer and maps the deep features back to the original image space via a single convolution, that is:
104	\begin{equation}
105	I _ {o u t} = {Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left({PUNE} \left(F _ {4}\right) + {Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left(I _ { {input}}\right)\right)\tag{3}
106	\end{equation}
107	where $I_{input}$ is the input image, $I_{out}$ is the final output denoised image, $Conv_{3\times3}(\cdot)$ denotes the convolution operation, $PUNE(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the PUNE layer.
108	\begin{figure*}[!t]
109	\centering
110	\includegraphics[width=7in]{Fig1}
111	\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{-8pt}  % 减小图片到图注的空白，按需修改‑6pt~‑12pt
112	\caption{Overall network architecture of the SAR-RWKV model, mainly composed of convolution operations, the patch-embedding layer, the RWKVM module, residual connections, and the patch-unembedding layer.}
113	\label{Fig1}
114	\end{figure*}
115	
116	Each RWKVM module consists of several cascaded RWKV-based Residual Sparse Transformer (RWKV Residual Sparse Transformer, RWKV-RST) modules, as shown in Fig. 2. Each RWKV-RST module comprises an RWKV module, a PUNE layer, a Convolutional Projection (CP) layer, and a PE layer. The CP layer consists of two 2-D convolutions with LeakyReLU activation plus one additional 2-D convolution, and is used for local refinement and nonlinear mapping of spatial features. The PE layer flattens the 2-D image features into a sequence to match the serialized input required by the subsequent RWKV module, while the PUNE layer restores the serialized features to a 2-D spatial structure at the original spatial scale.
117	\begin{figure*}[!t]
118		\centering
119		\includegraphics[width=6.3in]{Fig2}
120		\caption{Detailed network structure of the RWKV-RST module.}
121		\label{Fig2}
122	\end{figure*}
123	
124	The RWKV module is formed by connecting a Sparse Spatial Mix (SSM) module and a Channel Frequency Mixing (CFM) module in series, which are respectively responsible for sparse-attention modeling in the spatial dimension and nonlinear information interaction in the channel dimension, that is:
125	\begin{equation}
126		F _ {s p a} = F _ {k - 1} ^ {\prime} + S S M \left(F _ {k - 1} ^ {\prime}\right), \quad k = 1, 2, 3, 4\tag{4}
127	\end{equation}
128	\begin{equation}
129		F _ {f u s e} = F _ {s p a} + C F M \left(F _ {s p a}\right)\tag{5}
130	\end{equation}
131	where $F_{spa}$ is the output feature of the SSM module, $F_{fuse}$ is the output feature of the CFM module, and $F_{k-1}^{'}$ is the input of the RWKV-RST module. $SSM(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the SSM module, $CFM(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the CFM module.
132	
133	Finally, the RWKV-RST module obtains its final output feature through the PE, CP, and PUNE layers:
134	\begin{equation}
135		\begin{split}
136			F_{k}^{\prime} ={PUNE}\Bigl(
137			&{CP}\bigl({CP}\bigl({PE}(F_{{fuse}})\bigr)\bigr)\Bigr) + F_{k-1}^{\prime} ,\\
138			&\quad k = 1, 2, 3, 4
139		\end{split}
140		\tag{6}
141	\end{equation}
142	where $PE(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the PE layer, $CP(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the CP layer, $PUNE(\cdot)$ denotes the mapping function of the PUNE layer. We describe the specific network structures of the SSM and CFM modules are described next.
143	\vspace{-2pt}
144	\subsection{Sparse Spatial Mix Module}
145	To strengthen the model's response to critical sparse regions and improve the effectiveness of global modeling, we sparsify the RWKV structure and constructs the SSM module, as shown in Fig. 2. Specifically, the SSM module introduces a Top-K selection strategy on the key component, retaining only the spatial locations with strong responses in order to reduce redundant-feature interference and highlight salient regions. On this basis, the SSM module fuses the filtered features through a gating mechanism, achieving effective interaction between important local information and the global context. The SSM module thus improves the specificity of spatial-feature modeling while maintaining linear complexity, which helps preserve structural information and recover texture detail.
146	
147	The SSM module first applies layer normalization to the input, then feeds it to a spatial-shift module that shifts the receptive field, and further obtains the values $X_r$, $X_k$, and $X_v$  of the three components $R$, $K$, and $V$ of the RWKV core computation through linear mapping and activation, that is:
148	\begin{equation}
149	X _ {s h i f t} = Q S h i f t \left(L N \left(F _ {k - 1} ^ {\prime}\right), p\right)\tag{7}
150	\end{equation}
151	\begin{equation}
152	\left\{ \begin{array}{l} X _ {r} = \sigma \left( {Linear} \left(X - X _ { {shift}}\right)\right) \\ X _ {k} =  {Linear} \left(X - X _ { {shift}}\right) \\ X _ {v} =  {Linear} \left(X - X _ { {shift}}\right) \end{array} \right.\tag{8}
153	\end{equation}
154	
155	where $F_{k-1}^{'}$ denotes the input feature, $LN(\cdot)$ denotes layer normalization, and $QShift(\cdot)$ denotes the multi-head spatial-shift function whose shift distance (in pixels) determines the offset range of the receptive field. $Linear(\cdot)$ denotes the linear mapping and  $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the Sigmoid activation.
156	
157	To capture fine local structures in the image, the SSM module introduces a Top-K sparse gating mechanism based on importance scores. For each spatial location, the norm of the key vector is computed as the importance score, and the $k$  most representative locations are selected to participate in the subsequent computation, where $T$ is the total number of spatial locations, and $X_{r}(t)$ is the key vector at the $X_r(t)$ is the key vector at the $t$-th spatial location. The retention ratio $\rho^{(l)}$ is adaptively controlled by a learnable parameter $\alpha^{(l)}$:
158	\begin{equation}
159	\rho^ {(l)} = 0. 5 + 0. 4 5 \cdot \sigma \left(\alpha^ {(l)}\right)\tag{9}
160	\end{equation}
161	where $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the Sigmoid activation and  $l$ is the current layer index. This mapping constrains $\rho^{(l)}$ to the interval [0.5, 0.95].
162	
163	Let $L$ be the total number of network layers. To make layers of different depths attend to information of different granularities, the learnable parameter  $\alpha^{(l)}$  is initialized hierarchically by layer: shallow layers $(l < L/3)$0.90 to retain more detail, middle layers  $(L/3 \leq l < 2L/3)$ are set to 0.80 to balance detail and semantics, and deep layers $(l \geq 2L/3)$ are set to 0.70 to focus on high-level semantic representation. On this basis, we generate a binary sparse mask and apply it to the key vector: 
164	\begin{equation}
165	\tilde {X} _ {r} (t) = X _ {r} (t) \cdot \mathbf {1} \left[ t \in {TopK} \left(s _ {t}, k\right) \right]\tag{10}
166	\end{equation}
167	where $\mathbf{1}[\cdot]$ is the indicator function and the index set ${TopK}(s_{t},k)$ denotes the $k$ locations with the highest importance scores. To effectively suppress redundant responses at non-critical locations, the SSM module uses the masked key vector in place of the original key vector for the K and V components in the attention computation, so that the model focuses—while computing sparsely—on the local structures most discriminative for denoising, that is:
168	\begin{equation}
169	F _ {b} = B i W K V \left(\tilde {X} _ {r} (t), X _ {k}, X _ {v}\right)\tag{11}
170	\end{equation}
171	where $BiWKV(\cdot)$ denotes the bidirectional RWKV computation, which aggregates contextual information in both the forward and backward directions through an exponential-decay weighting mechanism to capture more complete long-range dependencies; see \cite{ref42} for its detailed formulation. Finally, the SSM module further enhances the feature $F_{b}$ through a layer normalization and linear mapping:
172	\begin{equation}
173	F _ {s s m} = F _ {k - 1} ^ {\prime} +  {Linear} \left(L N \left(F _ {b}\right)\right)\tag{12}
174	\end{equation}
175	where $LN(\cdot)$ denotes layer normalization and $Linear(\cdot)$ denotes the linear mapping. The output feature of the SSM module serves as the input to the CFM module.
176	\vspace{-2pt}
177	\subsection{Channel Frequency Mixing Module}
178	The CFM module consists of two branches (a channel-attention branch and an AFM branch), an Adaptive Feature Fusion (AFF) module, and a skip connection; its detailed network structure is shown in Fig. 2. The channel-attention branch first applies layer normalization, linear mapping, and an activation function to the input feature, and obtains the channel-attention feature through element-wise multiplication, that is:
179	\begin{equation}
180		\begin{split}
181			X_{{Channel}} = \sigma\Bigl(
182			&{Linear}\bigl(F_{ssm}\bigr)\Bigr) \odot{Linear}\bigl(\\
183			&\quad{ReLU}\bigl({Linear}\bigl(LN(F_{ssm})\bigr)\bigr)\bigr)
184		\end{split}
185		\tag{13}
186	\end{equation}
187	where $F_{ssm}$ denotes the input feature, $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication, $LN(\cdot)$  denotes layer normalization, $Linear(\cdot)$ denotes the linear mapping, $ReLU(\cdot)$ denotes the ReLU activation function, and  $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the Sigmoid activation function.
188	
189	The AFM branch can effectively enhance the network's texture-detail recovery. Its network structure is shown in Fig. 3. First, the branch uses the DCT to map the layer-normalized feature into the frequency domain, that is:
190	\begin{equation}
191	F = D _ {H} X D _ {W} ^ {T}\tag{14}
192	\end{equation}
193	where $D_{H}, D_{W}$ are the DCT coefficient matrices along the height and width dimensions, respectively, defined as:
194	\begin{equation}
195	D _ {i} [ k, n ] = \sqrt {\frac {2}{N}} \cos \left(\frac {\pi k (2 n + 1)}{2 N}\right), \quad k, n = 0, 1, \dots , i - 1\tag{15}
196	\end{equation}
197	where $i \in \{H, W\}$. This transform decomposes spatial information into different frequency components, where the low-frequency components correspond to global structural information and the high-frequency components correspond to texture-detail information.
