198	\begin{figure}[htbp]
199		\centering
200		\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Fig3}
201		\caption{Network structure of the AFM module.}
202		\label{Fig3}
203	\end{figure}
204	
205	The AFM module then evenly partitions the frequency-domain space into several frequency bands according to frequency distance and constructs a corresponding mask for each band. We divide the frequency-domain space into $K$  bands for hierarchical modulation. For a frequency coordinate $(h,W)$, the normalized frequency distance is defined as:
206	\begin{equation}
207	d (h, w) = \frac {\sqrt {(h / H) ^ {2} + (w / W) ^ {2}}}{\max _ {h , w} \sqrt {(h / H) ^ {2} + (w / W) ^ {2}}}\tag{16}
208	\end{equation}
209	
210	Based on the frequency distance, the mask of the  band can be expressed as: $k$-th $(k = 0,1,\dots,K - 1)$ band can be expressed as:
211	\begin{equation}
212	M _ {k} [ h, w ] = \left\{ \begin{array}{l l} 1, & \frac {k}{K} \leq d (h, w) <   \frac {k + 1}{K} \\ 0, &  {otherwise} \end{array} \right.\tag{17}
213	\end{equation}
214	where the last band contains all the remaining high frequencies: $M_{K-1}[h,w]=\mathbf{1}\left[d(h,w)\geq\frac{K-1}{K}\right]$
215	
216	On this basis, the AFM module introduces a lightweight frequency-gating network that generates content-adaptive dynamic gating weights after extracting channel statistics through global average pooling, convolution, and activation, that is:
217	\begin{equation}
218	G = \sigma \left({Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left({ReLU} \left({Conv} _ {3 \times 3} (G A P (X))\right)\right)\right)\tag{18}
219	\end{equation}
220	where $GAP(\cdot)$ denotes global average pooling, $Conv_{3\times3}(\cdot)$ denotes a convolution with a $3\times3$ kernel, $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes Sigmoid activation function, and $ReLU(\cdot)$ denotes the ReLU activation function.
221	
222	The AFM module then combines the dynamic gating weights with learnable static frequency weights to weight the features of each band differentially, obtaining the final band weights:
223	\begin{equation}
224	W = W _ { {static}} \odot G\tag{19}
225	\end{equation}
226	where $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication. Next, the AFM module applies the adaptive weights to the band-partitioned frequency features, aligning dimensions automatically through broadcasting, that is:
227	\begin{equation}
228	\tilde {F} = \sum_ {k = 0} ^ {K - 1} W _ {k} \otimes \left(F \odot M _ {k}\right)\tag{20}
229	\end{equation}
230	where $W_{k}$ is the weight of the $k$-th band, $F$ is the DCT coefficient matrix, $M_{k}$  is the band mask, $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication, and $\otimes$ denotes broadcast multiplication. Finally, the AFM module transforms the weighted frequency features back to the spatial domain through the Inverse Discrete Cosine Transform (IDCT) and obtains the final feature through a convolution and an activation function, that is:
231	\begin{equation}
232	X _ {A F M} = {Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left({GELU} \left({Conv} _ {3 \times 3} \left(D _ {H} ^ {T} \tilde {F} D _ {W}\right)\right)\right)\tag{21}
233	\end{equation}
234	
235	Through the above design, the AFM module can dynamically adjust its response strength to different frequency bands according to the input content, effectively retaining low-frequency structural information while enhancing the model's perception and recovery of high-frequency texture detail, thereby compensating for the limited detail expressiveness of purely spatial-domain modeling.
236	
237	To effectively integrate the complementary features extracted by the two branches (the channel-attention branch and the AFM branch), we design the AFF module, whose detailed network structure is shown in Fig. 4. Through the synergy of global and local gating, the module adaptively fuses the output features of the two branches in a weighted manner, avoiding the information redundancy and feature imbalance caused by simple addition or concatenation. Inspired by the squeeze-and-excitation network, global gating extracts the global channel statistics of the features through adaptive average pooling, generates channel-wise attention weights through two convolutional layers, and globally calibrates the concatenated features to suppress redundant channels and highlight discriminative ones. Meanwhile, local gating models the relative importance of the two features along the sequence dimension, generates position-wise fusion weights through convolution, normalizes them with a Sigmoid function modulated by a learnable temperature parameter, and sums the two globally calibrated features. Finally, the fused result is further refined and output through a residual-connected feature-enhancement layer. Through this co-design of global and local dual gating, the AFF module can adaptively balance the contributions of frequency and sparse-spatial features in both the channel and spatial dimensions, achieving more thorough feature fusion.
238	\begin{figure}[htbp]
239		\centering
240		\includegraphics[width=0.48\textwidth]{Fig4}
241		\caption{Network structure diagram of AFF module.}
242		\label{Fig4}
243	\end{figure}
244	
245	As shown in Figure 4, the AFF module first concatenates the output feature of the AFM branch $X_{AFM}$ and that of the channel-attention branch $X_{Channel}$ along the channel dimension, then computes the global gating weights through global average pooling, convolution operation, and activation function:
246	\begin{equation}
247		\begin{split}
248			G_{{global}} = \sigma\Bigl(
249			&{Conv}_{1\times1}\bigl(
250			{ReLU}( 
251			{Conv}_{1\times1}(\\	&\quad
252			{GAP}(
253			{Cat}(X_{{AFM}}, X_{{Channel}})
254			)
255			)\bigr)\Bigr)
256		\end{split}
257		\tag{22}
258	\end{equation}
259	where $GAP(\cdot)$ denotes global average pooling, $Cat(\cdot)$ denotes channel concatenation, $Conv_{1\times1}(\cdot)$ denotes a convolution operation with a $1\times1$ kernel, $ReLU(\cdot)$ denotes the ReLU activation function, and $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the Sigmoid activation. The AFF module then modulates the concatenated feature with the global gating weights and splits it back into channels:
260	\begin{equation}
261	X _ {F} ^ {\prime}, X _ {C} ^ {\prime} =  {Split} \left( {Cat} \left(X _ {A F M}, X _ { {Channel}}\right) \odot G _ { {global}}\right)\tag{23}
262	\end{equation}
263	where $Split(\cdot)$ denotes the channel-splitting operation.
264	
265	In addition, the AFF module employs convolution and activation operations to construct a local gating mechanism that modulates the output feature $X_{AFM}$ of the AFM branch and the feature $X_{Channel}$ of the channel-attention branch; that is, it learns position-wise fusion weights through 1D convolution and performs element-wise multiplication between these weights and the globally gated features:
266	\begin{equation}
267	A _ {F} = {Conv} _ {1 \times 1} \left({ReLU} \left({Conv} _ {1 \times 1} \left(X _ {A F M}\right)\right)\right)\tag{24}
268	\end{equation}
269	\begin{equation}
270	A _ {C} = {Conv} _ {1 \times 1} \left({ReLU} \left({Conv} _ {1 \times 1} \left(X _ { {Channel}}\right)\right)\right)\tag{25}
271	\end{equation}
272	\begin{equation}
273	X _ {f u s e} = A _ {F} \odot X _ {F} ^ {\prime} + A _ {C} \odot X _ {C} ^ {\prime}\tag{26}
274	\end{equation}
275	where $Conv_{1\times1}(\cdot)$ denotes a convolution operation with a $1 \times 1$ kernel, $\odot$ denotes element-wise multiplication, and $ReLU(\cdot)$ denotes the ReLU activation function.
276	
277	SAR-RWKV refines local detail through the RWKV-RST modules and models global structure through residual connections, ultimately achieving effective extraction of both global and local features and delivering strong denoising performance via a carefully designed frequency-domain feature-interaction mechanism. Moreover, the proposed adaptive feature-fusion mechanism can effectively combine global channel attention with local attention, achieving efficient feature fusion.
278	\vspace{-2pt}
279	\subsection{Loss Function}
280	In SAR image denoising, the design of the loss function is crucial to the quality of the reconstructed image. Although the mean-squared error is simple to compute, it easily causes the model to over-smooth during denoising, blurring local structures—especially edges and texture detail \cite{ref38}. To effectively train the proposed denoising network, we design a multi-constraint composite loss function that jointly considers reconstruction accuracy, robustness, and the structural priors of the image. The total loss function of the proposed model is as follows:
281	\begin{equation}
282	\mathcal {L} _ {t o t a l} = \lambda_ {M S E} \mathcal {L} _ {M S E} + \lambda_ {L 1} \mathcal {L} _ {L 1} + \lambda_ {M S T V} \mathcal {L} _ {M S T V}\tag{27}
283	\end{equation}
284	where $\lambda_{MSE}$, $\lambda_{L1}$, and $\lambda_{MSTV}$ are the weights of the Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss, the L1 loss, and the MSTV loss, respectively. Each loss term is defined below. The MSE loss is used mainly to minimize the pixel-level difference between the denoised and GT images, computed as:
285	\begin{equation}
286	\mathcal {L} _ {M S E} = \frac {1}{N} \sum_ {i = 1} ^ {N} \left| I _ {o u t} ^ {(i)} - I _ {g t} ^ {(i)} \right| ^ {2}\tag{28}
287	\end{equation}
288	
289	The L1 loss provides better robustness in noise suppression and is calculated as:
290	\begin{equation}
291	\mathcal {L} _ {L 1} = \frac {1}{N} \sum_ {i = 1} ^ {N} | I _ {o u t} ^ {(i)} - I _ {g t} ^ {(i)} |\tag{29}
292	\end{equation}
293	where $I_{out}$ denotes the denoised image, $I_{gt}$ denotes the GT, and $N$  is the total number of pixels. To maintain the piecewise-smooth property of the image while protecting edge structures, we introduce the MSTV loss, computed as:
294	\begin{equation}
295	\mathcal {L} _ {M S T V} = \sum_ {s = 1} ^ {S} \alpha_ {s} \cdot T V _ {s} \left(I _ { {out}}\right)\tag{30}
296	\end{equation}
297	where $s$ denotes the number of scales, $\alpha_s$ denotes the adaptive weight of the $s$-th scale, and $TV_s(\cdot)$ denotes the total-variation loss at the $s$-th  scale. By computing total variation at multiple scales, the MSTV loss effectively balances noise suppression against texture-detail preservation.
