299	\section{Experimental Results and Analysis}
300	\subsection{Datasets and Experimental Setup}
301	We randomly selected 1,000 images form the UC Merced Land Use (UMLU) dataset \cite{ref43} to serve as clean references. Because operational SAR products are almost always single-channel, we first convert the color images to grayscale and then inject multiplicative noise, yielding the simulated noisy-clean image pairs that we use for training. The test data include both synthetic and real datasets. For the synthetic SAR image tests, three datasets are used: (i) 100 images randomly selected from the remaining UMLU images, from which simulated SAR images with an Equivalent Number of Looks (ENL) of 1, 2, and 4 are synthesized; and (ii) the SAR9 and SSAR synthetic datasets released in \cite{ref44}, both of which contain noisy SAR images with ENLs of 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16 together with corresponding GT images, which let us evaluate denoising performance quantitatively. For the real-image tests, to further verify how well the proposed method generalizes to real SAR data, we randomly select four real SAR images from a variety of typical scenes. Fig. 5 shows these images, which we name SAR1–SAR4 for convenience. Fig. 5(a) is an image of size 256×256 with an ENL of 3. Fig. 5(b) is a TerraSAR-X image with an ENL of 1, a size of 256×256, and a resolution of 1 m×1 m. Fig. 5(c) is a KeyHole KH-7 image of size 511×511. Fig. 5(d) is a SAR image with an ENL of 3, a size of 401×401, and a resolution of 1 m×1 m.
