313	During training, the batch size is set to 4 and the initial learning rate to 1e-4, the network is trained for 200 epochs. The network is optimized using the Adam optimizer. In the training phase, the UMLU synthetic dataset is used for model training; each image is preprocessed into grayscale and cropped into 64×64 patches as model input. To improve generalization, we augment the training data with random rotations of 90°, 180°, and 270° and horizontal flipping. The experiments are conducted on Windows 11 with Python 3.9 and PyTorch 1.12.1, using two RTX 2080 Ti GPUs.
314	\vspace{-2pt}
315	\subsection{Evaluation Metrics and Comparison Algorithms}
316	For the synthetic datasets, GT is available, so we objectively evaluate each algorithm with the Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) \cite{ref45}, the Structural Similarity Index Measure (SSIM) \cite{ref45}, and the Feature Similarity Index Measure (FSIM) \cite{ref46}. Real images lack noise-free references, so, following \cite{ref47}, we adopt three no-reference metrics: ENL, the Unassisted Measure (UM), and the Ratio-of-Average Edge Preservation Degree (EPD-ROA), which are reported separately for the horizontal (HD) and vertical (VD) directions. The larger the PSNR and ENL, the better the performance of the denoising algorithm. The closer SSIM, FSIM, and EPD-ROA are to 1, the better the quality of the denoised image. Finally, a smaller UM indicates stronger overall denoising. 
317	
318	To verify the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm, we compare it on the synthetic and real datasets against the following SAR image denoising algorithms: SAR-BM3D \cite{ref16}, LG-DBNet \cite{ref32}, FRANet \cite{ref33}, SAR-Transformer \cite{ref34}, CLSAR \cite{ref35}, ACGNet \cite{ref36}, SDUDNet \cite{ref44}, G-MONet) \cite{ref48}, SARCAM \cite{ref49}. All comparisons use the code provided by the respective authors.
319	\vspace{-2pt}
320	\subsection{Denoising on Synthetic Datasets}
321	For the synthetic-dataset tests, we evaluate the proposed algorithm on three datasets: UMLU, SSAR, and SAR9. The denoised results are shown in Tables 1, 2, and 3. Each value we report is the mean of the PSNR, SSIM, and FSIM scores over all images in the dataset. Throughout, boldface marks the top score, and an underline marks the runner-up. Table 1 exposes clear trade-offs among the baselines: SAR-Transformer suppresses noise aggressively yet sacrifices edges; CLSAR and SARCAM do the reverse, guarding edges while removing less noise; and LG-DBNet lands between these two extremes. The remaining algorithms (AGSDNet, SAR-BM3D, FRANet, G-MONet, and SDUDNet) fare relatively poorly in this experiment. Overall, SAR-RWKV tops every metric, showing that it suppresses speckle effectively while better preserving texture detail. As Tables 2 and 3 show, SAR-RWKV achieves the highest PSNR, SSIM, and FSIM values on the SSAR and SAR9 synthetic datasets. This indicates that, even on small-scale synthetic datasets, SAR-RWKV can effectively suppress speckle while retaining image detail and structural information, exhibiting stronger overall denoising performance and further illustrating the robustness and generalization of the proposed model.
