390	To qualitatively compare the denoising performance of each algorithm, we randomly select one image from the SAR9 denoising results and magnify a local region, as shown in Fig. 6 (the image has an ENL of 2). Fig. 6 shows the denoising result of each algorithm together with its PSNR and SSIM values. A small amount of noise remains in the denoised images of FRANet, SARCAM, LG-DBNet, and ACGNet, indicating weak denoising in homogeneous regions. The results of G-MONet and CLSAR are blurred, with some loss of texture detail. SAR-BM3D, SAR-Transformer, and SDUDNet produce blurred edges, and SAR-BM3D introduces obvious artificial textures in denoised urban areas, whereas the proposed algorithm achieves better results. This shows that SAR-RWKV effectively solves the blooming of bright targets caused by global information aggregation and suppresses artificial textures. Although LG-DBNet attains the second-best PSNR, its speckle suppression in homogeneous regions is still incomplete, with residual speckle, whereas the proposed algorithm achieves better suppression in homogeneous regions—benefiting from its sparse-gated spatial-mixing RWKV module, which suppresses background noise over long ranges while better preserving local strong-scattering features. In addition, SAR-BM3D attains the second-best SSIM but exhibits obvious texture deformation in urban areas. The proposed algorithm's PSNR is about 0.13 dB higher than the second-best method's, and its SSIM is about 0.02 higher. In summary, the proposed algorithm can effectively suppress speckle while preserving texture and edge detail.
