595	\subsection{Analysis of Ratio Maps}
596	To better illustrate the visual quality of the denoised images, we visualize the ratio maps of the nine comparison methods and the proposed method in Figs. 11, 12, 13, and 14. The closer a method's ratio map is to pure noise, the better its denoising result. As Fig. 11 shows, for SAR1, the ratio maps of G-MONet and SARCAM contain substantial ground-object structure, while those of CLSAR, ACGNet, and SDUDNet show varying degrees of texture residue in built-up areas. The ratio map of the proposed method shows no obvious texture residue anywhere in the image, indicating better texture preservation—mainly due to the CFM module, which recovers texture well in the denoised image. As Fig. 12 shows, for SAR2, the ratio map of G-MONet contains substantial ground-object structure, while those of SDUDNet, LG-DBNet, and ACGNet contain substantial texture detail. SAR-BM3D, SAR-Transformer, LG-DBNet, and SARCAM show minor texture leakage in built-up areas, whereas FRANet, CLSAR, and the proposed method preserve texture relatively well in built-up areas. As Fig. 13 shows, for SAR3, the ratio maps of G-MONet and SARCAM again contain substantial ground-object structure, and those of SAR-Transformer, CLSAR, SDUDNet, LG-DBNet, and ACGNet contain residual texture detail across the image. The proposed method preserves texture best, followed by SAR-BM3D and FRANet. As Fig. 14 shows, for SAR4, the ratio map of G-MONet contains substantial ground-object structure, and those of SARCAM, SDUDNet, ACGNet, and SAR-Transformer show obvious texture residue, while SAR-BM3D, CLSAR, FRANet, and LG-DBNet show slight texture residue. The proposed method's ratio map shows almost no texture residue, exhibiting the best texture preservation. Overall, the proposed method is superior to the others in both denoising performance and texture preservation, effectively removing noise while retaining texture detail.
