662	\subsection{Ablation Experiment}
663	We ablated the individual modules on the SSAR and SAR9 datasets to quantify their contributes in Table 8, where we highlight the best metric in bold and the second-best with an underline. We designed three ablated models: (1) w/o SSM, which replaces the SSM module with a standard RWKV; (2) w/o AFM, which replaces the AFM module with a skip connection; and (3) w/o AFF, which replaces the AFF module with direct element-wise addition. The full model with all components attains the best objective metrics, so each module plays an indispensable role. Replacing the SSM module with RWKV significantly reduces PSNR and SSIM; without the SSM module, the model's response to critical sparse regions weakens, slightly reducing denoising performance. This shows that the SSM module contributes to recovering the texture detail of SAR images. Replacing the AFM module with a skip connection reduces PSNR and SSIM, especially on SSAR, indicating that the AFM module enhances the model's perception and recovery of high-frequency texture detail while effectively retaining low-frequency structural information, thereby compensating for the limited detail expressiveness of purely spatial-domain modeling. Replacing the AFF module with direct element-wise addition reduces the PSNR to 30.1233dB and the SSIM to 0.8301, indicating that the AFF module adaptively balances the contributions of frequency and sparse-spatial features and avoids information redundancy and feature imbalance. In summary, the SSM and AFM modules play key roles in recovering the global structure and detail of the image, while the AFF module balances the contributions of frequency and sparse-spatial features.
