683	To confirm that our loss design pays off, we ablated the loss function on the SSAR and SAR9 datasets, as shown in Fig. 15. We first fixed the L1-loss weight at 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8; then, taking the MSE loss weight as the horizontal axis and the MSTV-loss weight as the vertical axis (each set to 0, 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8), we computed and plotted the model's SSIM on the SSAR dataset. The model reaches optimum value when the L1-loss weight is 0.5, the MSE loss weight is 0.5, and the MSTV-loss weight is 0.005. To further highlight the advantage of the MSTV loss, we replaced it with the TV loss and repeated the ablation on the SSAR and SAR9 datasets, as shown in Table 9. On SSAR, the PSNR and SSIM of the MSTV loss are 30.1758 dB and 0.8464, respectively, both better than those of the TV loss. On SAR9, the MSTV loss shows the same trend, with its SSIM increasing by 2.61\%, fully demonstrating that the MSTV loss better overcomes the over-smoothing that the TV loss tends to produce and better preserves structural information in the denoised image.
