735	\subsection{Complexity Analysis}
736	To better illustrate model performance, we analyze the model complexity in terms of parameter count (Params) and floating-point operations (FLOPs), as reported in Table 11. SAR-RWKV has a clear advantage in parameter count—only 1.295 M, far below mainstream models such as SAR-Transformer (24.099 M) and FRANet (9.460 M), and only slightly higher than G-MONet and SDUDNet—mainly owing to the linear-complexity mechanism of RWKV, which avoids the quadratic overhead of self-attention while maintaining strong global-modeling capability. However, the FLOPs of SAR-RWKV reach 18.718 G, second only to FRANet, because the RWKV module must scan recursively position by position along the spatial dimension to obtain a global receptive field; although this does not markedly increase the parameter count, the serialized computation still incurs high floating-point overhead. Overall, SAR-RWKV exhibits a "low parameter count, relatively high computation" profile, reflecting a trade-off that spends computation to save parameters. It has deployment advantages in storage-constrained scenarios, but its inference-stage computational cost should be further reduced through strategies such as parallel scanning.

