\begin{abstract}
The coherent manner in which Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) forms an image unavoidably imprints speckle onto it, and this noise sharply lowers image quality. Current global-modeling denoisers, however, over-smooth isolated strong scatterers and fail to cleanly separate speckle from high-frequency texture in the spatial domain. To address these problems, we introduce the Receptance Weighted Key Value (RWKV) mechanism into the field of SAR image denoising and propose a SAR image denoising algorithm based on a sparse-attention-enhanced RWKV network (SAR-RWKV). First, to solve the blooming of high-brightness targets caused by global information aggregation, SAR-RWKV constructs a sparse-gated spatial-mixing RWKV module that dynamically locks onto and isolates important high-coherence point targets through a Top-K mechanism, achieving long-range background denoising while better preserving local strong-scattering features. Second, to address the severe aliasing between speckle and real terrain details in the spatial domain, a Channel Frequency Mixing (CFM) module is designed for SAR-RWKV, which maps features into the frequency domain through the discrete cosine transform. The sparsity of the frequency-domain representation is exploited to decouple speckle noise from texture, and the frequency components are adaptively weighted according to the content, significantly enhancing the restoration of image texture details. In addition, a Multi-Scale Total Variation (MSTV) loss is incorporated into SAR-RWKV to overcome the over-smoothing caused by the traditional total variation loss. Experiments on multiple SAR image datasets show that SAR-RWKV outperforms a variety of state-of-the-art methods in both quantitative and qualitative evaluations.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Frequency-domain feature enhancement, multiscale total variation, receptance weighted key value (RWKV), sparse attention, speckle reduction, synthetic aperture radar (SAR) image.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section*{Note to Practitioners}
\textit{Radar satellites and airborne radar sensors can survey the ground at night and through cloud, which makes them valuable for flood mapping, crop and forest monitoring, infrastructure inspection, and search-and-rescue planning. Their pictures, however, carry a grainy interference pattern that hides small objects and blurs boundaries, so operators often inspect scenes by hand or discard data. This work was motivated by the need for an automatic cleanup step that removes the graininess without erasing the very features an analyst is looking for. Existing cleanup tools tend to trade one against the other: aggressive filters flatten fields and roads into featureless patches and smear bright metallic objects such as ships, vehicles, and towers into blobs, whereas conservative filters leave enough residual grain to confuse downstream detection software. Our method treats the two problems separately. It first singles out the small number of picture locations that carry strong returns and protects them while cleaning the surrounding background, and it then separates interference from genuine texture by examining the picture in terms of its frequency content rather than pixel by pixel. On both simulated and real radar images, this produces cleaner backgrounds and sharper edges than nine published methods. The model is also compact, at roughly 1.3 million parameters, so it fits on modest hardware and suits on-board or field deployment. Its main limitation is speed: the sequential scan it uses to gather context across a whole picture makes it computationally heavier than its size suggests, and its smoothness over uniform areas remains slightly below that of the most aggressive filters. Future work will parallelize this scan for real-time operation, extend the approach to multichannel and polarimetric radar, and adapt it to other imaging systems that suffer from similar interference, such as ultrasound and optical coherence tomography.}
\section{Introduction}
\IEEEpubidadjcol
\IEEEPARstart{A}{s} an active microwave remote sensing imaging technology, Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) has been widely used in environmental monitoring, disaster assessment, military reconnaissance, resource exploration, and other fields, owing to its all-day, all-weather imaging capability and its ability to penetrate clouds and fog \cite{ref1,ref2}. Compared with optical remote sensing, SAR can obtain high-quality surface information under severe weather conditions, providing key data support for time-sensitive emergency response \cite{ref3}. However, the coherent imaging mechanism of SAR systems introduces speckle noise, which seriously hinders the interpretation of ground information. Speckle is an inherent physical phenomenon arising from the coherent superposition of randomly distributed scatterers within a resolution cell \cite{ref4}. It appears as a spatially correlated grainy random pattern with multiplicative and non-stationary statistical properties, which severely degrades the radiometric resolution and visual interpretability of images \cite{ref5,ref6}. Speckle noise not only blurs object edges and geometric contours but also destroys texture and structural information, significantly affecting the performance of downstream tasks such as object detection \cite{ref7,ref8}, terrain classification \cite{ref9}, and change detection \cite{ref10}. Therefore, how to effectively suppress speckle noise while recovering more image details has long been a core challenge in the field of SAR image processing.

